1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Row versions and snapshots. How PostgreSQL lets a reader and a writer share one row

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,500
xmin is the transaction that inserted this version: 733 (illustrative IDs).

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,800
xmax = 0: no deleting or replacing transaction is recorded.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,800
The UPDATE adds a replacement version and stamps 735 into the old one's xmax.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:24,200
Not two tickets: two versions of one logical row, each with visibility rules.

6
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:27,700
A transaction sees its own changes: A reads high.

7
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:30,700
B reads the last committed version, low. It does not wait for A.

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:38,100
xmax = 735 alone doesn't hide the old version: 735 has not committed.

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:44,600
In Read Committed (the default), each statement takes a new snapshot: B now sees high.

10
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:48,100
A Repeatable Read transaction keeps the snapshot from its first statement: still low.

11
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:52,000
A's commit was not lost. It is outside that older snapshot; a new transaction sees high.

12
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:59,100
While that old snapshot is open, the low version must stay. Long transactions delay cleanup.

13
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:03,600
Now no snapshot needs the low version. It is a dead row version.

14
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:07,100
VACUUM (usually autovacuum) marks that space reusable. Routine maintenance, not a malfunction.

15
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:10,600
MVCC is not a history log. To keep what changed, record it on purpose, like ticket_events.

16
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:22,200
Remember: An UPDATE writes a new row version; it does not overwrite in place. xmin = inserting transaction, xmax = replacing (or locking) one. Each read sees the version its snapshot allows. Read Committed: new snapshot per statement. Repeatable Read: per transaction. VACUUM reclaims old versions once no snapshot needs them.

